<h2 id="ephemeral-environments-are-inefficient-in-traditional-postgres" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Ephemeral Environments Are Inefficient in Traditional Postgres</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#ephemeral-environments-are-inefficient-in-traditional-postgres" aria-label="Link to Ephemeral Environments Are Inefficient in Traditional Postgres"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<hr/>
<section class="mt-8 mb-20 flex flex-col gap-y-3 md:mb-12"><div class="flex justify-end gap-x-2.5"><button class="flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center border border-gray-new-80 bg-white transition-colors duration-200 hover:bg-gray-new-94 dark:border-gray-new-20 dark:bg-gray-new-10 dark:hover:bg-gray-new-15" type="button"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 20 20" class="rotate-180 text-gray-new-40 dark:text-white"><path stroke="#fff" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-width="1.455" d="m8 5 5 5-5 5"></path></svg><span class="sr-only">Prev</span></button><button class="flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center border border-gray-new-80 bg-white transition-colors duration-200 hover:bg-gray-new-94 dark:border-gray-new-20 dark:bg-gray-new-10 dark:hover:bg-gray-new-15" type="button"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 20 20" class="text-gray-new-40 dark:text-white"><path stroke="#fff" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-width="1.455" d="m8 5 5 5-5 5"></path></svg><span class="sr-only">Next</span></button></div><div draggable="false" style="opacity:1;transform:none;-webkit-touch-callout:none;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;touch-action:pan-y"><section class="quote my-8 border-l-2 border-green-44 pl-6"><figure class="flex flex-col gap-5"><blockquote class="max-w-[710px] border-none p-0 font-mono text-xl leading-snug font-normal tracking-extra-tight text-black-pure dark:text-white sm:text-lg">&quot;<!-- -->Getting realistic data into our verification environments was largely unfeasible, it was time-consuming, expensive, and a beast to maintain. You need to process hefty backups, transfer costs stack up, and there’s a lot of manual oversight required just to move that data.<!-- -->&quot;</blockquote><figcaption class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2.5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2.5"><div class="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-base leading-snug tracking-tight"><span class="font-medium text-black-pure dark:text-white">Jonathan Reyes</span><span class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 dark:text-gray-new-70">—</span><cite class="font-normal text-gray-new-40 not-italic dark:text-gray-new-70">Principal Engineer at Dispatch</cite></div></div><a class="group inline-flex w-fit items-center gap-1 sm:wrap-anywhere shrink-0 text-base leading-none font-medium tracking-tight text-black-pure no-underline dark:text-white" href="/blog/how-dispatch-speeds-up-development-with-neon-while-keeping-workloads-on-aurora"><span>Read case study</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="12" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 16 12" class="-mb-px size-3.5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 group-hover:translate-x-[3px] text-gray-new-40 dark:text-gray-new-70"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-width="1.4" d="M11.108 10.921 15 7.03l-3.892-3.892M.938 7.032h12.728"></path></svg></a></figcaption></figure></section></div></section>
<p><strong>Provisioning instances is slow. Once they&#x27;re live, you have to babysit them</strong>. New instances have to be configured, they take a while to be available, and once running, they need constant oversight to ensure they are appropriately sized and ready.</p>
<p><strong>You pay for non-prod instances 24/7 even if you only use them for a few hours</strong>. Production databases stay on 24/7, but this is not the case for dev/test instances. But in other serverless databases, unless you manually pause them, you&#x27;ll keep paying even if they&#x27;re not running.</p>
<p><strong>It&#x27;s hard to keep data in sync across environments</strong>. Syncing data across many instances requires repetitive, manual work. This leads to discrepancies that compromise test reliability and slow down deployments.</p>
<p><strong>These problems get worse over time, not better</strong>. As your number of instances grows, the manual setup and configuration work grows too.</p>
<p><span aria-owns="rmiz-modal-" data-rmiz=""><span data-rmiz-content="not-found" style="visibility:visible"><img alt="Persistent environments in AWS RDS" loading="lazy" width="704" height="447" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" class="rounded-lg" style="color:transparent;width:100%;height:100%" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Faws-rds-environments.jpg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 1x, /_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Faws-rds-environments.jpg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7 2x" src="/_next/image?url=%2Fuse-cases%2Faws-rds-environments.jpg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75&amp;dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7"/><span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 z-10 border border-black mix-blend-overlay dark:border-white rounded-lg" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></span></p>
